[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Interfaces and Abstract Classes in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In C#, both interfaces and abstract classes are used to achieve abstraction and polymorphism, but they have distinct characteristics and use cases. Understanding their differences is crucial for designing robust and maintainable applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Interfaces"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An interface defines a contract that classes can implement. It specifies a set of methods, properties, events, and indexers that implementing classes must provide. Interfaces cannot contain implementation details; they only define the signatures."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Interfaces are declared using the 'interface' keyword."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"All members of an interface are implicitly public."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A class can implement multiple interfaces."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Interfaces cannot contain fields."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"interface IDrawable
{
    void Draw();
}

interface IResizable
{
    void Resize(int width, int height);
}

class Circle : IDrawable, IResizable
{
    public void Draw()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Drawing a circle");
    }

    public void Resize(int width, int height)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Resizing circle to width: {width}, height: {height}");
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the 'Circle' class implements both 'IDrawable' and 'IResizable' interfaces, providing concrete implementations for the 'Draw' and 'Resize' methods."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Abstract Classes"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"An abstract class is a class that cannot be instantiated directly. It can contain both abstract and non-abstract members. Abstract members must be implemented by derived classes, while non-abstract members provide default implementations that derived classes can inherit or override."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Abstract classes are declared using the 'abstract' keyword."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Abstract methods are declared using the 'abstract' keyword and have no implementation in the abstract class."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A class can inherit from only one abstract class."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Abstract classes can contain fields, constructors, and destructors."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"abstract class Shape
{
    public abstract double Area();

    public virtual string Description()
    {
        return "This is a shape.";
    }
}

class Rectangle : Shape
{
    public double Width { get; set; }
    public double Height { get; set; }

    public override double Area()
    {
        return Width * Height;
    }

    public override string Description()
    {
        return "This is a rectangle.";
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'Shape' is an abstract class with an abstract method 'Area' and a virtual method 'Description'. The 'Rectangle' class inherits from 'Shape' and provides implementations for both methods. The 'Area' method *must* be implemented, while 'Description' is overridden."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inheritance: A class can implement multiple interfaces but can inherit from only one abstract class."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implementation: Interfaces cannot provide implementation details, while abstract classes can provide partial implementations."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Members: Interfaces can only contain method signatures, properties, events, and indexers. Abstract classes can contain fields, constructors, and destructors."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"When to Use Which"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use interfaces to define a contract that multiple unrelated classes can implement. This promotes loose coupling and allows for greater flexibility."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use abstract classes when you want to provide a common base class with some default implementation and enforce a certain structure for derived classes. This is useful when you have a clear "is-a" relationship between classes."},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"In summary, interfaces define *what* a class should do, while abstract classes define *how* a class should do it (at least partially). Choosing the right approach depends on the specific requirements of your application."}]
